; NextDAAD --tile-slack A/B fixture (2026-08-07, rev 1).
;
; Compiled and staged by tests\build-tests.ps1 -TileSlack, which also runs
; tests\video\tileslack_ab.py to produce the four encodes and copies
; build\nextdaad.nex in. sd\TILESLK\ is emptied and refilled on every run
; and holds nothing but GAME.DDB, 001-004.VID and NEXTDAAD.NEX - copy the
; folder to the card and launch NEXTDAAD.NEX from inside it.
;
; RUN SHEET: docs\superpowers\tileslack-ab-run-sheet.md. Read it before
; running; it says what to look for on each pair and what a given outcome
; means.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; --tile-slack is the encoder's one opt-in picture knob. The manual carries
; a benchmark table for it (four rows: two clips at 0.0 and 0.5, with
; columns for rungs taken, stream utilisation, local 4x4 PSNR and a banding
; index) and tests\video\tileslack_ab.py re-measures every cell of it.
;
; THIS FIXTURE ANSWERS THE HALF NO METRIC ANSWERS: what the clip LOOKS
; like, moving, on a real ZX Spectrum Next. The owner's own stock footage
; banded and juddered on kit defaults after the whole fixture deck passed,
; so a metrics-improved verdict does not settle whether the picture
; improved. A still panel cannot judge a MOTION artifact and neither can a
; PSNR figure.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IS STAGED - TWO A/B PAIRS, ONE VARIABLE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;   001.VID   PAIR 1  boat pan     ARM A  --tile-slack 0.0
;   002.VID   PAIR 1  boat pan     ARM B  --tile-slack 0.5
;   003.VID   PAIR 2  church zoom  ARM A  --tile-slack 0.0
;   004.VID   PAIR 2  church zoom  ARM B  --tile-slack 0.5
;
; Both pairs are `full` 320x256 @25, Layer 2 mode-1, encoded from
; authoring-kit\VIDEO\001.mp4 and 002.mp4 - the whole clip in each case.
; Within a pair NOTHING differs but the knob: same source, same cut, same
; shape, same fps, same dither, same retime, same everything else, and the
; STREAM BUDGET IS PINNED on arm B to the value arm A derived, so the
; finer tile rung cannot move its own supply ceiling and flatter itself.
;
; The four files are the SAME BYTES the measurement script measured - the
; staging step copies its cached output rather than re-encoding, so the
; numbers on the page and the picture on the glass are the same encode.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; BOTH ARMS OF A PAIR CAN LOOP, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every arm has a play-once verb and a LOOPING verb (any key ends a loop).
; The predecessor card (.superpowers\sdd\sp14a-task-4-report.md section
; 43.2) staged ONE looping arm against three play-once ones and had to warn
; that the asymmetry cut in the looping arm's favour, because a looping
; clip gives the eye repeated passes. Here the two arms of a pair are
; equally easy to stare at, so that correction is not needed.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE LABEL - the owner must never have to guess which arm is playing
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Layer 2 sits ABOVE the tilemap and a 320x256 video covers the whole
; surface, so no caption can be visible DURING playback. The label is
; therefore printed twice, either side of it:
;
;   BEFORE  the arm is named in full and the fixture waits for a key, so
;           playback starts only once the label has been read;
;   AFTER   DISPLAY 1 clears the Layer 2 plane (l2_clear walks 10 pages in
;           mode 1, src\overlay2.asm) and the same label is reprinted under
;           "JUST PLAYED", followed by the menu.
;
; If the text does NOT come back after a clip, the plane did not clear -
; that is a Layer 2 fault, not a video one, and the run sheet says so.
;
; NO PICTURE, NO SOUND ASSETS, NO MUSIC. Nothing is staged but the four
; encodes, so a symptom cannot be a stale asset winning the picture probe
; chain, a song's interrupt hold, or an audio interaction. The clips carry
; their own audio inside the NXV stream.

; System flags used by name (DAAD BLANK template spellings)
#define fPlayer             38
#define fVerb               33

/CTL
_
/VOC
NORTH  2  noun
SOUTH  3  noun
LOOK   4  verb
; The A/B verbs. The digit in each name IS the --tile-slack value, so the
; command the owner types already says which arm it is: 0 = arm A (0.0),
; 5 = arm B (0.5). An L prefix means LOOP.
MENU  80  verb
PAN0  81  verb
PAN5  82  verb
LPAN0 83  verb
LPAN5 84  verb
ZOM0  85  verb
ZOM5  86  verb
LZOM0 87  verb
LZOM5 88  verb
/STX
/0  "It's too dark to see anything."
/1  "I can also see:"
/2  "What now?"
/3  "What next?"
/4  "What should I do now?"
/5  "What should I do next?"
/6  "Not a command here - type MENU for the list."
/7  "I can't go in that direction."
/8  "Not a command here - type MENU for the list."
/9  "I have with me:"
/10 "I am wearing:"
/11 " "
/12 "Are you sure?"
/13 "Another go?"
/14 " "
/15 "OK."
/16 "Press any key."
/17 " "
/18 " "
/19 " "
/20 " "
/21 " "
/22 " "
/23 "I'm not wearing one of those."
/24 "I can't. My hands are full."
/25 "I already have the _."
/26 "There isn't one of those here."
/27 "I can't carry any more things."
/28 "I don't have one of those."
/29 "I'm already wearing the _."
/30 "Y"
/31 "N"
/32 "More..."
/33 ">"
/34 " "
/35 " "
/36 "I now have the _."
/37 "I'm now wearing the _."
/38 "I've removed the _."
/39 "I've dropped the _."
/40 "I can't wear the _."
/41 "I can't remove the _."
/42 "I can't remove the _. My hands are full."
/43 "The _ weighs too much for me."
/44 "The _ is in the"
/45 "The _ isn't in the"
/46 ", "
/47 " and "
/48 ".#n"
/49 "I don't have the _."
/50 "I'm not wearing the _."
/51 "."
/52 "There isn't one of those in the"
/53 "Nothing."
/54 " "
/55 " "
/56 " "
/57 "I/O Error"
/58 " "
/59 "File name error."
/60 "Type in name of file."
/MTX
/0 "NEXTDAAD TILE-SLACK A/B FIXTURE"
; Messages 1-4 are the ARM LABELS. Each names the pair, the clip, the arm,
; the exact encoder option and the file, so a photograph of the screen is
; enough to say which encode was on the glass.
/1 "PAIR 1  BOAT PAN     ARM A  --tile-slack 0.0  (001.VID)"
/2 "PAIR 1  BOAT PAN     ARM B  --tile-slack 0.5  (002.VID)"
/3 "PAIR 2  CHURCH ZOOM  ARM A  --tile-slack 0.0  (003.VID)"
/4 "PAIR 2  CHURCH ZOOM  ARM B  --tile-slack 0.5  (004.VID)"
/5 "LOOPS - PRESS ANY KEY TO STOP IT"
/6 "PLAYS ONCE"
/7 "Press any key to start."
/8 "JUST PLAYED"
/9 "COMMANDS#nPAN0   PAN5    boat pan, once      (arm A / arm B)#nLPAN0  LPAN5   boat pan, loops#nZOM0   ZOM5    church zoom, once   (arm A / arm B)#nLZOM0  LZOM5   church zoom, loops#nMENU           this list again#nThe digit is the --tile-slack value: 0 = arm A, 5 = arm B.#nWatch each pair BACK TO BACK, never from memory."
/OTX
/0 "a brass lamp"
/LTX
/0 ""
/1 ""
/CON
/0
NORTH 1
/1
SOUTH 0
/OBJ
/0    1   1   _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _   _ _
/PRO 0
; Main loop. Location 0 is the "not set up yet" state: the first pass
; builds the window and prints the menu, then moves to location 1 so every
; later pass is just the parser.
> _ _   AT 0
        PROCESS 6
        GOTO 1
> _ _   PROCESS 1
/PRO 1
; Turn loop, the gmodegate shape: parse, run the verb table, complain if
; nothing matched. There is no map and no object handling to do.
> _ _   PARSE 0
        REDO
> _ _   PROCESS 5
        ISDONE
        REDO
> _ _   NEWTEXT
        LT fVerb 14
        SYSMESS 7
        REDO
> _ _   SYSMESS 8
        REDO
/PRO 5
; ---- THE VERB TABLE - eight arms, two pairs, one shape each ----
; Every entry is the same nine steps and they only differ in WHICH label
; message and WHICH video number they name:
;
;   PROCESS 7   clear the screen, print the fixture title
;   MESSAGE n   the arm label (1-4)
;   INK/MESSAGE  LOOPS (5) or PLAYS ONCE (6)
;   PROCESS 8   "press any key to start", then wait
;   GFX n 13    play once      /  GFX n 14   loop, any key ends it
;   PROCESS 9   clear Layer 2, print "JUST PLAYED"
;   MESSAGE n   the SAME arm label, so the screen still names what you
;               just watched
;   PROCESS 10  the menu
;
; GFX n 13/14 is the documented player dispatch surface (h_gfx sub 13/14,
; src\overlay2.asm -> vid_play, src\video.asm) - the same one the -Vid and
; -VidLong legs drive.

; ---- PAIR 1: boat pan ----
> PAN0    _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 1
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 6
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 1 13
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 1
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> PAN5    _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 2
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 6
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 2 13
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 2
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> LPAN0   _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 1
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 5
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 1 14
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 1
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> LPAN5   _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 2
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 5
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 2 14
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 2
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

; ---- PAIR 2: church zoom ----
> ZOM0    _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 3
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 6
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 3 13
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 3
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> ZOM5    _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 4
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 6
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 4 13
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 4
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> LZOM0   _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 3
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 5
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 3 14
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 3
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> LZOM5   _     PROCESS 7
                MESSAGE 4
                INK 13
                MESSAGE 5
                PROCESS 8
                GFX 4 14
                PROCESS 9
                MESSAGE 4
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> MENU    _     PROCESS 7
                PROCESS 10
                DONE

> LOOK    _     PROCESS 7
                PROCESS 10
                DONE
/PRO 6
; One-shot setup. The text window is the WHOLE tilemap - there is no
; picture window here, because a 320x256 video owns the entire Layer 2
; surface while it plays and the menu owns the whole screen while it does
; not.
;
; MODE 2 disables the "More..." pager for this window (h_mode writes
; WIN_FLAGS bit 1, src\overlay0.asm). Without it the menu would page.
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        WINAT 0 0
        WINSIZE 32 80
        MODE 2
        PAPER 0
        INK 7
        CLS
        PROCESS 7
        PROCESS 10
/PRO 7
; Screen header. Called before every arm and by MENU/LOOK.
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        CLS
        INK 15
        MESSAGE 0
        NEWLINE
        INK 14
/PRO 8
; The gate before playback: the label above has been printed, so the clip
; cannot start before it has been read.
> _ _   NEWLINE
        INK 7
        MESSAGE 7
        ANYKEY
/PRO 9
; Post-playback. DISPLAY 1 clears the Layer 2 plane to the transparent
; index (h_display's non-zero arm -> l2_clear, src\overlay2.asm) so the
; tilemap under it is readable again - without it the clip's last frame
; would sit over the text. The tilemap itself is never touched by the
; player, so the reprint below is the only thing that writes it.
> _ _   DISPLAY 1
        WINDOW 1
        CLS
        INK 15
        MESSAGE 8
        INK 14
/PRO 10
; The menu.
> _ _   NEWLINE
        INK 7
        MESSAGE 9
/END
